  "prompt_topico_vazio": "[fill in the Title field above with the subject/topic]",
  "prompt_notebooklm": "You are in a notebook with SOURCES ALREADY LOADED. Generate flashcards EXCLUSIVELY from those sources.\n\nCONTEXT:\nSubject/Topic: {topico}\n\nMOST IMPORTANT RULE — DO NOT GO BEYOND THE SOURCES:\nDo not use your own knowledge, do not supplement with what you remember about the topic, and do not invent numbers, deadlines or percentages that aren't written in the sources. If the sources don't cover the topic, reply only: NOT ENOUGH SOURCE — and write no card at all. A card invented to look right is worse than no card: it enters the deck, gets memorized as true, and is only disproven on exam day.\n\nFORMAT (one card per line, with a BLANK LINE between cards):\nQuestion :: Answer :: tags\n\nRULES:\n1. Each card tests ONE idea and stands alone — whoever reviews it in two months won't have the sources in front of them. No \"according to the text\", \"per the source\" or \"in the document\".\n2. The answer is the CONCEPT, never where it's located — don't copy a page number or \"per section X\" as if it were the answer.\n3. For blanks, use {{c1::term}} — each blank in the SAME card with a different number (c1, c2, c3).\n4. Tags: optional, 1-3 lowercase words without spaces (use _), comma-separated.\n5. END the answer with the citation in parentheses: (Source: <document name>, <section/page>) — that citation is what lets you check the card later instead of trusting it blindly.\n6. Reply ONLY with the cards: no intro, no numbering, no comments of your own.\n\nHOW MANY: as many as the sources support without repeating the same idea — don't force a fixed number.",
