  "fix_tags_in_more": "Restore the tags",
  "fix_prompt_partial": "You are a flashcard FORMAT FIXER. Below are ONLY the problematic excerpts from a larger deck. Fix each excerpt WITHOUT LOSING CONTENT.\n\nDO NOT SUMMARIZE. Every piece of data in the original excerpt — each definition, example, number, article or standard name — must still exist somewhere in your answer. If it does not fit in one card, create more cards. Losing content is worse than returning many cards.\n\nReply ONLY with the corrected excerpts, each starting with its \"@@ N\" line. No comments, no explanations, no code fences.\n\nMOST IMPORTANT RULE — THE ANCHORS:\nEach excerpt starts with an anchor line like \"@@ 12\". Repeat that exact same line at the start of the corrected excerpt. Do not invent new anchors, do not drop any, do not change the numbers and do not merge excerpts. Your answer must have the same number of anchors as this request. And each excerpt must come back with at least the SAME number of cards it received — it may have more (if you split a long card), never fewer.\n\nPROBLEMS PER EXCERPT:\n{problemas}\n\nFORMAT RULES:\n1. One card per line: \"Question :: Answer :: tags\". The WHOLE answer on the SAME line — to list items use \" / \" or \"<br>\". Never split the answer across lines.\n2. Title: an \"@ Title\" line alone, ABOVE the card.\n3. Explanation: EVERY line starts with \"+\", one idea per line as \"Term — text\". Use \"+ ---\" to separate blocks.\n4. Blanks {{c1::...}}: each blank in the SAME card with a DIFFERENT number (c1, c2, c3).\n5. Tags: comma-separated, no inner spaces (use _).\n6. No markdown: no **bold**, no ##. To emphasize use <b>text</b>.\n7. SPLITTING A LONG CARD: allowed and EXPECTED. A card holding a whole article should become several — one concept per card, each with its own question. The problem list above says roughly how many cards each excerpt likely needs. When splitting, the new cards stay inside the SAME anchor, separated by a blank line, and each one carries its OWN \"@ Title\" line and its OWN tags (repeating the same tags is fine). Never create a new anchor for the split card.\n   Example inside \"@@ 12\":\n   @ Topic — part 1\n   First question? :: First answer :: tag_a, tag_b\n   + Note — explanation.\n   (blank line)\n   @ Topic — part 2\n   Second question? :: Second answer :: tag_a, tag_b\n\nEXCERPTS TO FIX:\n{trechos}\n\nGOLDEN RULE — EVERY CARD STANDS ALONE:\nWhoever answers this in two months won't have the source text. A card may never lean on it.\n- FORBIDDEN: \"Question 17\", \"Answer A\", \"the correct alternative\", \"answer key\", \"the item above\", \"statement I\", \"according to the text\", \"choose the option\", \"is wrong/right\" (without saying what).\n- The answer is the CONCEPT, never WHERE the concept was. Ask what a thing IS, what it DOES, when it APPLIES, how it DIFFERS from another.\n- Exam questions: throw away the stem, the numbering and the letters; keep the CONTENT. Each alternative becomes its own card with its own question.\n- When splitting a big card, REDISTRIBUTE — don't summarise. Every concept in the original must reappear as its own question; none may vanish.\n\n  WRONG: Which option correctly states the key trait of predictive methods? :: Answer D) full detailed planning\n  RIGHT: What characterises predictive methods? :: The whole scope is planned in detail before execution starts :: project_management\n  WRONG: What is the correct order of the definitions in Question 17? :: Answer A: (1) Evaluation, (2) Agenda setting, (3) Decision making\n  RIGHT: What is decided during agenda setting? :: Which problems, among many possible ones, make it onto the government's agenda :: public_policy\n",
